\begin{abstract}

The increasing deployment of UAV-assisted vehicular networks introduces new
dependability challenges due to highly dynamic connectivity, fragmented
observations, and the vulnerability of beacon-based communication to
falsified information attacks. Existing trust-management approaches often
evaluate observations independently and fail to preserve accumulated trust
evidence when vehicles transition between heterogeneous monitoring regions.

This paper proposes a mobility-aware multi-UAV edge--cloud trust-management
framework that enables continuous trust assessment across UAV coverage
boundaries. The proposed framework integrates multi-source physical evidence
fusion, Bayesian trust evolution, guarded CUSUM-based temporal suspicion
tracking, Bayesian conservative confirmation, and adaptive trust
enforcement. A cross-UAV trust-state continuity mechanism preserves
decision-relevant trust information, including Bayesian trust parameters,
temporal suspicion statistics, and warning history, during vehicle mobility.

The proposed framework is evaluated using ns-3-based simulations under
multiple falsified-beacon attack scenarios, communication degradation
conditions, and scalability settings. In the evaluated default attack
configuration, the framework achieves a detection rate of 96.19\% with zero
observed false positives over 15 independent simulation seeds, with a 70\%
malicious vehicle ratio and 50\% beacon falsification probability. Dedicated
component ablations demonstrate that preserving cross-UAV trust-state
continuity increases detection rate from 41.90\% to 96.19\%, while integrating
guarded CUSUM-based temporal verification increases detection rate from
71.43\% to 96.19\% without increasing observed false-positive decisions.
Under 20\% packet loss, the framework maintains 91.43\% detection performance
with zero observed false positives within the evaluated simulations.
Compared with a clean-safe local segment-reset
VeReMi-style baseline, the proposed approach achieves a 12.38 percentage
point improvement under degraded communication conditions by retaining
historical trust evidence across UAV transitions.

The results demonstrate that combining mobility-aware trust-state continuity
with temporal evidence verification can improve dependability in simulated
UAV-assisted vehicular network scenarios.

\end{abstract}

