\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{C}{onnected} vehicular networks are increasingly expected to support safety-critical services such as cooperative awareness, collision avoidance, traffic coordination, and infrastructure-assisted autonomous driving. In these environments, vehicles periodically exchange beacon messages containing timestamp, position, speed, heading, lane, and other mobility-related information. The correctness of these messages is essential because neighboring vehicles, roadside infrastructure, UAV-assisted monitors, and edge servers may use them to make time-sensitive decisions. However, the open and wireless nature of vehicular communication makes beacon messages vulnerable to falsification attacks, including position falsification, speed manipulation, timestamp inconsistency, stealthy falsification, and adaptive misbehavior. A malicious vehicle can therefore degrade cooperative perception, mislead trust decisions, and reduce the dependability of intelligent transportation systems.

Misbehavior detection in vehicular networks has been studied extensively, especially after the introduction of reproducible benchmarks such as VeReMi \cite{veremi}. Existing approaches include plausibility checks, physical-consistency validation, machine-learning-based classifiers, federated learning models, and trust-management mechanisms \cite{andradeSalazar2022VCADS,ilango2022NPFADS,campos2024FLMisbehavior,siddiqui2021SurveyTrustIoV}. Physical-consistency detectors are particularly attractive for safety-critical vehicular systems because they exploit interpretable relationships among beacon fields, such as timestamp continuity, feasible displacement, speed consistency, heading behavior, and lane-level plausibility. However, most such detectors are primarily message-centric or field-centric. They evaluate whether a received beacon is physically plausible, but they do not fully address how suspicion should persist when the monitoring authority itself changes due to mobility.

This limitation becomes important in UAV-assisted vehicular edge networks. UAVs and distributed edge nodes can extend coverage, reduce latency, and support safety-critical vehicular applications in regions where fixed infrastructure is sparse or overloaded \cite{vehFogSurvey2023,hao2024UAVVehicularFRL}. Nevertheless, vehicle mobility causes a natural handover problem: a vehicle may move from the coverage of one UAV/edge node to another while its suspicious behavior is still being accumulated. Without a trust-state continuity mechanism, evidence observed before the transition is not incorporated into subsequent trust evolution at the receiving monitor. This fragmentation can weaken consistent decision making, especially when packet loss or overlap-region ambiguity further disrupts the evidence stream.

The handover problem has been studied in vehicular edge and MEC systems, but mainly from the perspective of service continuity, task migration, latency, packet delivery, and application-state transfer \cite{qi2021SocialHandover,monir2022SeamlessHandover,ali2024EdgeFogFederation}. Similarly, recent work on collaborative misbehavior detection considers knowledge or model transfer among infrastructure nodes \cite{sedar2024KnowledgeTransfer}. These studies demonstrate that cross-edge coordination is feasible and useful. However, transferring application state or learned model knowledge is different from transferring security-relevant trust state. A partially accumulated suspicion score can affect punitive decisions such as warning or demotion; therefore, it must be transferred cautiously. Blind transfer may amplify false punishment, while no transfer may allow attackers to evade detection after handover.

Trust-management research provides another important foundation. Bayesian trust models, dynamic thresholding, adaptive trust evaluation, and temporal change-point techniques such as CUSUM have been used to improve robustness against uncertainty, on--off behavior, and non-stationary attacks \cite{siddiqui2021SurveyTrustIoV,abidi2025CAMSI,comert2021change,liu2025QART}. These approaches show that trust should not be decided from isolated observations. Instead, evidence should be accumulated over time and converted into actions only when sufficient confidence is available. However, most trust models still assume a relatively stable monitoring domain or a fixed trust authority. They do not explicitly address how Bayesian trust evidence, temporal anomaly scores, and safety guards should behave when vehicles cross UAV/edge coverage boundaries.

To address these challenges, this paper proposes a mobility-aware multi-UAV
edge--cloud trust-management framework that enables continuous trust
assessment across UAV coverage boundaries. The proposed framework combines
physical-consistency evidence extraction, Bayesian trust evolution, guarded
CUSUM-based temporal suspicion accumulation, Bayesian conservative
confirmation, and adaptive trust enforcement.

Unlike approaches that independently evaluate observations at each monitoring
node, the proposed framework introduces cross-UAV trust-state continuity,
where decision-relevant trust information is transferred during vehicle
mobility. This enables receiving UAV edge nodes to utilize previously
accumulated behavioral evidence instead of initiating independent security
decisions from fragmented observations.

Existing studies have significantly advanced falsified-information detection
in vehicular networks; however, most approaches assume continuous observation
from a fixed monitoring entity or evaluate abnormal behavior using isolated
decision windows. Such assumptions become unrealistic in UAV-assisted
vehicular networks, where vehicles continuously migrate across dynamic UAV
coverage regions. As a result, behavioral evidence collected by one UAV may
become fragmented when the vehicle enters another UAV monitoring region,
causing loss of historical trust information and inconsistent security
decisions.

Therefore, the remaining challenge is not only detecting anomalous vehicle
behavior, but also maintaining trustworthy decisions under mobility-induced
observation fragmentation. A dependable trust-management framework for
multi-UAV vehicular networks must preserve security-relevant evidence across
UAV boundaries, accumulate temporal suspicion reliably, and enforce decisions
without introducing unnecessary false positives.

The major contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}

\item A mobility-aware multi-UAV edge--cloud trust-management architecture
is proposed for falsified-beacon detection in dynamic vehicular networks.
The architecture integrates distributed UAV edge intelligence with
cloud-assisted trust analytics while maintaining lightweight edge decision
capability, realized through per-beacon evidence extraction and Bayesian
trust updating (Algorithm~\ref{alg:evidence_bayesian}).

\item A cross-UAV trust-state continuity mechanism is introduced to preserve
decision-relevant security evidence during vehicle mobility. Unlike
traditional handover mechanisms that primarily maintain communication
connectivity, the proposed approach maintains trust information required for
consistent security decisions across UAV boundaries
(Algorithm~\ref{alg:handover_transfer}).

\item A guarded temporal verification mechanism based on CUSUM is integrated
with Bayesian trust evolution and conservative confirmation to improve
detection reliability while preventing premature trust degradation and false
demotions (Algorithm~\ref{alg:cusum_confirmation}).

\item A comprehensive evaluation framework is developed using ns-3-based
simulation experiments covering attack diversity, packet-loss robustness,
component ablations, baseline comparison, and scalability analysis. The
results demonstrate improved robustness while maintaining zero observed
false positives across the evaluated safety scenarios, enforced online
through the adaptive trust enforcement procedure (Algorithm~\ref{alg:trust_decision}).

\end{itemize}

The evaluation investigates whether mobility-aware trust continuity improves decision reliability when evidence is fragmented by UAV handover and communication loss. In the primary handover ablation, the cross-UAV trust-state continuity mechanism substantially improves malicious-vehicle detection by preserving accumulated behavioral evidence during UAV transitions, without increasing observed false-positive decisions. The guarded CUSUM and confirmation mechanisms further support temporal evidence accumulation while preserving safety. Under clean honest conditions, the framework avoids false demotion, confirming that the decision logic is conservative. Packet-loss and attack-variant experiments further show that the proposed system maintains strong detection for several physical falsification attacks, while timestamp-only manipulation remains more sensitive to communication loss. This limitation is important because it suggests that timing-dominant attacks should be treated as first-class cases in future vehicular misbehavior benchmarks.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~II presents the
related work and identifies the research gap. Section~III introduces the
system model and problem formulation. Section~IV describes the proposed
mobility-aware trust-management framework. Section~V presents the experimental
methodology. Section~VI discusses the experimental results. Section~VII
discusses limitations and future work. Section~VIII concludes the paper.


