\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

This section reviews prior work most relevant to the proposed mobility-aware multi-UAV edge--cloud trust-management framework. The discussion is organized around five research streams: vehicular misbehavior detection, trust accumulation and temporal anomaly detection, UAV/edge-assisted vehicular handover, collaborative and cross-edge misbehavior detection, and safety-preserving trust enforcement. The purpose is not only to list existing techniques, but to clarify the gap addressed in this work.

\subsection{Vehicular Misbehavior Detection and Physical-Consistency Methods}
\label{subsec:rw_misbehavior_detection}

Misbehavior detection (MBD) in cooperative intelligent transportation
systems has been extensively studied. Existing approaches mainly focus
on identifying abnormal vehicle behaviour through physical consistency
checks, statistical analysis, or learning-based models. Comprehensive
surveys have categorized MBD approaches into data-centric and
node-centric detection strategies while highlighting challenges related
to evaluation consistency and reproducibility~\cite{vdh_survey}.

The VeReMi dataset provides a common benchmark for evaluating
misbehavior detection algorithms by providing attack traces and
ground-truth labels~\cite{veremi}. Its extension further expands the
benchmark by introducing additional attack types, sensor errors, and
realistic evaluation conditions~\cite{veremi_ext}.

A large body of work has since focused on physical-consistency and plausibility-based detection. These methods examine whether reported beacon fields are mutually consistent with the vehicle's recent motion history. For example, position, speed, heading, timestamp, and acceleration constraints can be used to identify messages that violate basic mobility feasibility. VCADS is a representative physics-based misbehavior detection system that checks whether Basic Safety Message fields are consistent with physically derivable relationships among independent beacon attributes \cite{andradeSalazar2022VCADS}. Such detectors are attractive because they are interpretable, lightweight, and well aligned with the semantics of vehicular beacons.

Machine learning approaches have also been applied for detecting
false information attacks. For example, Ercan et al. developed
machine-learning-based detection models for position falsification
attacks using vehicular network data, demonstrating improved
classification capability under benchmark scenarios~\cite{ercan_ml}.
NPFADS targets novel position-falsification attacks in the Internet of
Vehicles and evaluates detection using standard classification metrics such
as precision, recall, F1-score, ROC, and PR behavior
\cite{ilango2022NPFADS}. Federated learning has also been applied to
VeReMi-style misbehavior detection to reduce centralized data dependence and
improve privacy preservation \cite{campos2024FLMisbehavior}. These studies
show that stronger classifiers can improve detection performance under
benchmarked attack settings.

However, most physical-consistency and learning-based detectors remain primarily message-centric or field-centric. They answer the question: ``Is this beacon physically or statistically suspicious?'' They do not fully answer the mobility-aware trust-management question: ``How should suspicion persist, transfer, and be enforced when the observing UAV/edge monitor changes?'' In a multi-UAV vehicular edge environment, this distinction is important because the detector itself may change as vehicles move across UAV coverage regions. Therefore, strong beacon-level detection alone is not sufficient to guarantee robust trust decisions under handover and packet loss.

\subsection{Trust Management, Bayesian Evidence Accumulation, and Temporal Detection}
\label{subsec:rw_trust_temporal}

Trust-management approaches aim to move beyond isolated message classification by maintaining node-level trust or reputation over time. Survey studies on Internet-of-Vehicles trust management emphasize that prior trust models often depend on manually selected weights, static thresholds, and inconsistent attack-specific evaluation \cite{siddiqui2021SurveyTrustIoV}. This motivates more adaptive and probabilistic trust models that can operate under uncertainty.

The Bayesian trust model estimates vehicle reliability based on
positive and negative observations. The formulation follows the Beta
reputation framework, where positive and negative evidence update the
parameters of a Beta distribution, and the posterior expectation is
used as the trust estimate~\cite{josang2002}. Infrastructure-assisted
trust models such as CAMSI combine multi-source information, Bayesian
inference, dynamic weighting, and probabilistic priors to support
cooperative ITS trust evaluation \cite{abidi2025CAMSI}. Such work reinforces
the value of infrastructure-side trust fusion, especially when evidence is
incomplete or noisy.

CUSUM is used to accumulate suspicious evidence over time. Originally
introduced as a sequential change detection method, CUSUM detects
persistent deviations by accumulating small changes over consecutive
observations~\cite{page1954}. Recent cyber-physical security studies have
further demonstrated the effectiveness of CUSUM-based sequential anomaly
detection under dynamic operating conditions~\cite{zhang2019cusum}.
Change-point and sequential detection
methods, including CUSUM, have also been used for real-time cyberattack
detection in connected-vehicle environments \cite{comert2021change}. These
methods are useful when individual observations are weak but persistent
deviations reveal malicious behavior over time. Adaptive trust-threshold
methods, including Q-learning-based schemes, also address the trade-off
between malicious-vehicle detection and false alarms \cite{liu2025QART}.

Despite these advances, existing trust and temporal-detection methods often assume a fixed monitoring authority or a stable observation domain. They accumulate evidence over time, but they do not fully specify how Bayesian trust parameters, temporal suspicion scores, warning history, or confirmation status should be transferred when a vehicle moves from one UAV/edge monitor to another. This is the gap addressed by the proposed handover-aware trust-transfer and discounting mechanism.

\subsection{UAV-Assisted Vehicular Edge Computing and Handover}
\label{subsec:rw_uav_edge_handover}

UAVs have recently been considered as mobile monitoring platforms
for vehicular networks. Kerrache et al. investigated UAV-assisted
detection of malicious and selfish nodes in VANETs, demonstrating the
benefit of aerial observation for infrastructure-sparse environments
~\cite{kerrache_uav}. Recent UAV-assisted hierarchical trust-management
frameworks have further explored aerial-assisted trust evaluation and
cooperative security enforcement in vehicular
environments~\cite{uavtrust2024}. However, these approaches do not explicitly
address preservation of trust state during UAV handover transitions.

UAV-assisted vehicular edge networks have received
increasing attention because UAVs can extend coverage, provide flexible edge
support, and improve service availability in infrastructure-limited or
overloaded regions. Fog and edge computing surveys for vehicular networks
emphasize the importance of low-latency computation, distributed resource
management, heterogeneity, and quality of service \cite{vehFogSurvey2023}.
UAV-assisted vehicular systems have also been studied for critical real-time
communication and adaptive edge intelligence \cite{hao2024UAVVehicularFRL}.

Handover is a central issue in mobile vehicular edge systems. Social prediction-based handover methods use trajectory prediction, sojourn-time estimation, signal strength, bandwidth, and cost to reduce ping-pong effects and handover failures in collaborative-edge vehicular networks \cite{qi2021SocialHandover}. MEC/SDN-based vehicular handover schemes address service continuity by coordinating task migration across neighboring RSUs or edge servers \cite{monir2022SeamlessHandover}. Edge--fog federation studies further show that authentication state and application state can be transferred across domains to reduce service interruption during mobility \cite{ali2024EdgeFogFederation}.

These studies demonstrate that mobility-aware state transfer is technically feasible and important. However, their primary focus is service continuity, authentication continuity, latency, packet delivery, or task migration. Security-relevant trust state is different from ordinary application state because it may trigger punitive actions such as warning or demotion. Therefore, trust-state transfer requires conservative handling: insufficient transfer allows attackers to evade detection after handover, while overly aggressive transfer can amplify false punishment. This paper focuses specifically on this security-oriented state-transfer problem.

\subsection{Collaborative and Cross-Edge Misbehavior Detection}
\label{subsec:rw_collaborative_detection}

Collaborative edge-based misbehavior detection has recently been explored to improve robustness across infrastructure nodes. Knowledge-transfer approaches for collaborative misbehavior detection among RSUs show that edge monitors can share useful model knowledge while avoiding negative transfer from untrusted sources \cite{sedar2024KnowledgeTransfer}. This is an important development because it recognizes that detector knowledge should not be transferred blindly across infrastructure nodes.

Nevertheless, transferring learned model knowledge is different from transferring real-time vehicle-level suspicion state. Model transfer improves detector training or adaptation, whereas operational trust transfer affects immediate decisions about a specific vehicle. In the proposed setting, a partially suspicious vehicle may cross from one UAV zone to another before any individual UAV has enough evidence to demote it. Therefore, the central challenge is not only collaborative learning, but safe continuation of vehicle-specific trust evaluation across monitor boundaries.

\subsection{Safety-Preserving Trust Enforcement}
\label{subsec:rw_safety_enforcement}

False positive control is important in safety-critical vehicular
applications because incorrect vehicle isolation can affect system
reliability. This requirement is also reflected in ETSI ITS security
recommendations for misbehavior detection and reporting mechanisms and IEEE
1609.2 security specifications for vehicular communication
systems~\cite{etsi2020,ieee1609}. Aggressive thresholds may increase detection but can also
falsely punish honest vehicles, especially under packet loss, overlap-region
ambiguity, or short observation windows. False demotion of honest vehicles
can reduce cooperation, degrade system dependability, and undermine trust in
the defense mechanism itself.

The literature increasingly recognizes this trade-off through adaptive thresholds, probabilistic trust fusion, and conservative confirmation strategies \cite{siddiqui2021SurveyTrustIoV,abidi2025CAMSI,liu2025QART}. However, overlap-region safety remains less developed. In UAV or RSU overlap areas, partial observations from multiple monitors can create inconsistent or weakly supported decisions. A BAN decision made from such weak evidence can create false punishment. The proposed overlap BAN guard addresses this issue by downgrading weak overlap-region BAN decisions to WARN unless they are supported by sufficiently strong physical or trust evidence.

