\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:system_model}

\subsection{Network Model}
\label{subsec:network_model}

We consider a UAV-assisted vehicular edge network in which connected vehicles travel along a road segment monitored by multiple UAV edge nodes. Let
\(\mathcal{V}=\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_N\}\) denote the set of vehicles and
\(\mathcal{U}=\{u_1,u_2,\ldots,u_M\}\) denote the set of UAV edge nodes. Each UAV is responsible for receiving vehicular beacons within its coverage zone and performing local trust evaluation. In the default configuration, two UAV edge zones are used to model a handover scenario in which vehicles transition from one monitoring region to another.

Each UAV edge node is connected to an edge--cloud coordination layer. The edge nodes perform local evidence extraction and trust updates, while the coordinator supports cross-zone trust-state transfer when a vehicle moves from one UAV coverage region to another. This architecture reflects a distributed vehicular trust-management setting in which decisions cannot rely only on a single static observer.

\subsection{Vehicle Beacon Model}
\label{subsec:beacon_model}

Each vehicle periodically broadcasts a mobility beacon containing its reported state. The beacon generated by vehicle \(v_i\) at time \(t\) is represented as

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_i(t)=\{i, \tau_i(t), x_i(t), y_i(t), s_i(t), \theta_i(t), \ell_i(t), b_i(t)\},
\label{eq:beacon_model}
\end{equation}

where \(i\) is the vehicle identifier, \(\tau_i(t)\) is the reported timestamp, \(x_i(t)\) and \(y_i(t)\) are the reported position coordinates, \(s_i(t)\) is the reported speed, \(\theta_i(t)\) is the reported heading, \(\ell_i(t)\) is the reported lane, and \(b_i(t)\) denotes braking status. The braking indicator is defined as

\begin{equation}
b_i(t)=
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{vehicle braking},\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:braking_status}
\end{equation}

The UAV that receives \(\mathcal{B}_i(t)\) compares the current beacon with previously observed beacons from the same vehicle. This comparison produces physical and temporal consistency evidence. For example, large position displacement over a short time interval, mismatch between speed and displacement, abnormal timestamp progression, or inconsistent lane behavior can indicate falsification.

\subsection{Multi-UAV Edge Observation Model}
\label{subsec:multi_uav_observation}

Let \(z_i(t)\in\mathcal{U}\) denote the UAV zone observing vehicle \(v_i\) at time \(t\). As vehicles move along the road, \(z_i(t)\) may change. Therefore, the observation sequence of a vehicle may be split across multiple UAVs:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_i = \mathcal{B}_i^{u_1} \cup \mathcal{B}_i^{u_2} \cup \cdots \cup \mathcal{B}_i^{u_M},
\label{eq:fragmented_observation}
\end{equation}

where \(\mathcal{B}_i^{u_j}\) is the set of beacons from vehicle \(v_i\) observed by UAV \(u_j\). A purely local detector that does not share state across UAVs can only use \(\mathcal{B}_i^{u_j}\) within its own segment. This causes evidence fragmentation because suspicious behavior may be distributed across UAV zones.

This fragmentation is central to the problem studied in this work. When distributed UAV monitors operate without trust-state continuity, observations collected across handover boundaries are evaluated as disconnected evidence segments. The proposed framework addresses this limitation through mobility-aware trust-state transfer.

\subsection{Handover Model}
\label{subsec:handover_model}

A handover event occurs when a vehicle moves from the coverage region of one UAV edge node to another. Let \(u_a\) be the source UAV and \(u_b\) be the target UAV. A handover event for vehicle \(v_i\) is represented as

\begin{equation}
h_i: u_a \rightarrow u_b.
\label{eq:handover_event}
\end{equation}

During handover, the source UAV may have accumulated trust evidence about \(v_i\). If this evidence is discarded, the target UAV must restart observation from an uninformed state. To avoid this, the proposed framework transfers the vehicle trust state from \(u_a\) to \(u_b\). However, the transferred state is discounted to reduce over-dependence on stale or context-specific evidence.

Let \(T_i^{u_a}(t)\) denote the trust state of vehicle \(v_i\) at the source UAV before handover. The target UAV initializes its trust state using a discounted version of the source state:

\begin{equation}
T_i^{u_b}(t^+) = \mathcal{D}_{\delta}\left(T_i^{u_a}(t^-)\right),
\label{eq:handover_discount}
\end{equation}

where \(\mathcal{D}_{\delta}(\cdot)\) is the handover trust-discounting function and \(\delta\) is the discount factor. In this work, \(\delta=0.30\) is used as the final conservative setting based on sensitivity analysis.

\subsection{Threat Model}
\label{subsec:threat_model}

The network contains both honest and malicious vehicles. Honest vehicles report their mobility state consistently, subject only to normal communication effects such as packet loss or reception variation. Malicious vehicles falsify one or more beacon fields to mislead the trust system. The attacker may manipulate timestamp, position, speed, or other mobility-related attributes.

The attacker is assumed to control only its own transmitted beacon content. It does not compromise the UAV edge nodes, the edge--cloud coordinator, or the trust-management software. The attacker also does not directly alter the raw observations received from honest vehicles. This threat model focuses on falsified-information attacks at the vehicular message level rather than infrastructure compromise.

Let \(\mathcal{M}\subset\mathcal{V}\) denote the set of malicious vehicles and \(\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{M}\) denote the set of honest vehicles. The trust framework must demote vehicles in \(\mathcal{M}\) while preserving vehicles in \(\mathcal{H}\).

\subsection{Trust State and Decision Model}
\label{subsec:trust_decision_model}

For each vehicle \(v_i\), the system maintains a trust state \(T_i(t)\). This state summarizes accumulated evidence, Bayesian trust parameters, warning history, CUSUM suspicion score, handover status, and confirmation information. Based on this state, the framework outputs one of three trust actions:

\begin{equation}
a_i(t) \in \{\text{SAFE}, \text{WARN}, \text{BAN}\}.
\label{eq:trust_action}
\end{equation}

A \(\text{SAFE}\) decision indicates normal behavior. A \(\text{WARN}\) decision indicates suspicious behavior that requires continued monitoring. A \(\text{BAN}\) decision indicates sufficiently confirmed malicious behavior. In this work, BAN corresponds to the final demotion action recorded as \texttt{demoted=YES} in the evaluation logs.

The use of WARN before BAN is intentional. It prevents isolated or weak evidence from immediately causing punishment and allows the system to accumulate stronger evidence before final demotion.

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{subsec:problem_definition}

The objective is to design a mobility-aware multi-UAV trust-management framework that detects malicious vehicles while minimizing false demotion of honest vehicles under handover and packet-loss conditions.

The detection objective is to maximize the detection rate

\begin{equation}
DR = \frac{TP}{TP+FN}\times 100,
\label{eq:problem_dr}
\end{equation}

where \(TP\) is the number of malicious vehicles correctly demoted and \(FN\) is the number of malicious vehicles that remain undemoted.

The safety objective is to minimize the false-positive rate

\begin{equation}
FPR = \frac{FP}{FP+TN}\times 100,
\label{eq:problem_fpr}
\end{equation}

where \(FP\) is the number of honest vehicles falsely demoted and \(TN\) is the number of honest vehicles correctly preserved.

Thus, the problem can be stated as follows:

\begin{equation}
\max DR \quad \text{subject to} \quad FPR \approx 0,
\label{eq:objective}
\end{equation}

under mobility-induced evidence fragmentation, UAV handover, packet loss, and heterogeneous falsification attacks.

This formulation emphasizes that the proposed system is not merely a local physical anomaly detector. It is a distributed trust-management framework that must accumulate evidence over time, transfer trust across UAV zones, confirm suspicious behavior conservatively, and avoid unsafe demotion of honest vehicles.

