\subsection{Overview}
\label{subsec:framework_overview}

The proposed framework is designed to detect falsified vehicular beacons in a mobile multi-UAV edge environment while avoiding unsafe demotion of honest vehicles. The framework differs from a conventional physical-consistency detector in two ways. First, it does not make final decisions from isolated beacon-level violations. Instead, it accumulates evidence over time using Bayesian trust updates and guarded CUSUM-based suspicion tracking. Second, it preserves trust continuity across UAV handover events by transferring discounted trust state from the source UAV to the target UAV.

For each received beacon, the UAV edge node performs five operations: physical-consistency evidence extraction, Bayesian trust update, guarded temporal accumulation, confirmation-based decision making, and handover-aware trust transfer. The final action for a vehicle is selected from \(\{\text{SAFE}, \text{WARN}, \text{BAN}\}\). A WARN action represents suspicious but not yet sufficiently confirmed behavior, while a BAN action represents confirmed malicious behavior.

Let \(T_i(t)\) denote the trust state of vehicle \(v_i\) at time \(t\). The trust state is represented as

\begin{equation}
T_i(t)=\{\alpha_i(t),\beta_i(t),C_i(t),n_i(t),w_i(t),z_i(t)\},
\label{eq:trust_state}
\end{equation}

where \(\alpha_i(t)\) and \(\beta_i(t)\) denote benign and suspicious Bayesian evidence supports, respectively, \(C_i(t)\) is the guarded CUSUM score, \(n_i(t)\) is the number of valid observations, \(w_i(t)\) is the warning history, and \(z_i(t)\) is the current UAV zone.

\subsection{Cloud Intelligence Layer}
\label{subsec:cloud_intelligence}

The proposed multi-UAV edge--cloud trust-management framework includes a
cloud-based intelligence layer that functions as a \emph{global aggregator
and policy repository}. In the current experiments, the cloud passively
collects UAV-local trust states, historical observations, and aggregated
metrics from multiple edge nodes. It disseminates global policies and
situational awareness information but \textbf{does not directly influence
per-UAV real-time trust computations}.

This design ensures that all measured detection rates, false-positive rates,
and ablation outcomes reflect UAV-edge intelligence. The cloud layer is
designed to support future extensions, where it may actively adjust trust
policies, perform global anomaly detection, or optimize cross-UAV handover
decisions in real-time.

\textbf{Note:} In all experimental results reported in this paper, the cloud
operates as a passive aggregator. Its potential active role is reserved for
future work and not included in the quantitative evaluations.

\subsection{Physical-Consistency Evidence Extraction}
\label{subsec:physical_evidence}

For each received beacon \(\mathcal{B}_i(t)\), the edge node extracts physical and temporal consistency evidence by comparing the reported state with the previously observed state of the same vehicle. The evidence vector is defined as

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e}_i(t)=
[e_i^{\tau}(t), e_i^{p}(t), e_i^{s}(t), e_i^{\theta}(t), e_i^{\ell}(t)],
\label{eq:evidence_vector}
\end{equation}

where \(e_i^{\tau}(t)\), \(e_i^{p}(t)\), \(e_i^{s}(t)\), \(e_i^{\theta}(t)\), and \(e_i^{\ell}(t)\) represent timestamp, position, speed, heading, and lane-consistency evidence, respectively.

The position residual is computed by comparing the reported displacement with the displacement expected from the reported speed and elapsed time. Let \(\Delta t=\tau_i(t)-\tau_i(t-1)\) and

\begin{equation}
d_i(t)=\sqrt{(x_i(t)-x_i(t-1))^2+(y_i(t)-y_i(t-1))^2}.
\label{eq:observed_displacement}
\end{equation}

A position-consistency residual can be expressed as

\begin{equation}
r_i^{p}(t)=\left|d_i(t)-s_i(t)\Delta t\right|.
\label{eq:position_residual}
\end{equation}

Similarly, a speed-consistency residual is obtained by comparing the reported speed with the speed implied by successive positions:

\begin{equation}
r_i^{s}(t)=\left|s_i(t)-\frac{d_i(t)}{\Delta t}\right|.
\label{eq:speed_residual}
\end{equation}

A beacon is considered suspicious when one or more residuals exceed their corresponding tolerance thresholds. The resulting evidence is not directly converted into a final BAN decision. Instead, it is passed to the Bayesian and CUSUM modules for guarded accumulation.

\subsection{Bayesian Trust Update}
\label{subsec:bayesian_update}

The Bayesian trust module accumulates benign and suspicious evidence for each
vehicle. The trust state is modeled using two evidence counters:
\(\alpha_i(t)\) for benign support and \(\beta_i(t)\) for suspicious support,
following Bayesian reputation-based trust modeling
principles~\cite{josang2002}. Given the extracted evidence vector
\(\mathbf{e}_i(t)\), the edge node computes a suspicious-evidence score
\(q_i(t)\in[0,1]\). A larger value of \(q_i(t)\) indicates stronger evidence
of falsification.

The Bayesian evidence update is defined as

\begin{equation}
\alpha_i(t)=\alpha_i(t-1)+\left(1-q_i(t)\right),
\label{eq:alpha_update}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\beta_i(t)=\beta_i(t-1)+q_i(t).
\label{eq:beta_update}
\end{equation}

The posterior maliciousness estimate is then computed as

\begin{equation}
P_i^{m}(t)=\frac{\beta_i(t)}{\alpha_i(t)+\beta_i(t)}.
\label{eq:malicious_probability}
\end{equation}

This formulation allows the framework to avoid overreacting to a single suspicious beacon. A vehicle becomes increasingly suspicious only when suspicious evidence accumulates over time.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:evidence_bayesian} summarizes the per-beacon evidence extraction and Bayesian trust update.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Physical-Consistency Evidence Extraction and Bayesian Trust Update}
\label{alg:evidence_bayesian}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Beacon $\mathcal{B}_i(t)$, previous state $(x_i(t-1),y_i(t-1),s_i(t-1))$, trust state $(\alpha_i(t-1),\beta_i(t-1))$
\STATE Compute $\Delta t \leftarrow \tau_i(t)-\tau_i(t-1)$
\STATE Compute observed displacement $d_i(t)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:observed_displacement}}
\STATE Compute position residual $r_i^{p}(t)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:position_residual}}
\STATE Compute speed residual $r_i^{s}(t)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:speed_residual}}
\STATE Assemble evidence vector $\mathbf{e}_i(t) \leftarrow [e_i^{\tau}(t),e_i^{p}(t),e_i^{s}(t),e_i^{\theta}(t),e_i^{\ell}(t)]$
\STATE Derive suspicious-evidence score $q_i(t)\in[0,1]$ from $\mathbf{e}_i(t)$
\STATE Update $\alpha_i(t) \leftarrow \alpha_i(t-1)+(1-q_i(t))$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:alpha_update}}
\STATE Update $\beta_i(t) \leftarrow \beta_i(t-1)+q_i(t)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:beta_update}}
\STATE Compute $P_i^{m}(t) \leftarrow \beta_i(t)/(\alpha_i(t)+\beta_i(t))$
\RETURN $q_i(t)$, $P_i^{m}(t)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Guarded CUSUM-Based Suspicion Accumulation}
\label{subsec:cusum_accumulation}

To address the limitation of isolated beacon decisions, the proposed
framework incorporates guarded CUSUM-based temporal evidence accumulation.
Instead of immediately classifying a vehicle based on a single suspicious
beacon, the mechanism accumulates consecutive suspicious observations over
time.

For vehicle \(v_i\), the guarded CUSUM score is updated as

\begin{equation}
C_i(t)=\max\left(0, C_i(t-1)+q_i(t)-\nu\right),
\label{eq:cusum_update}
\end{equation}

where \(\nu\) is a drift parameter that prevents normal small variations from accumulating indefinitely. A CUSUM alert is generated when

\begin{equation}
C_i(t)\geq H,
\label{eq:cusum_alert}
\end{equation}

where \(H\) is the CUSUM threshold.

Fig.~\ref{fig:cusum} illustrates the guarded CUSUM temporal evidence
accumulation mechanism. Consecutive suspicious observations progressively
increase the cumulative suspicion score until the adaptive threshold is
reached.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig5_cusum_ablation.pdf}
\caption{Conceptual representation of guarded CUSUM temporal evidence
accumulation. The figure explains how consecutive suspicious observations
