The CUSUM module is guarded by minimum-observation and evidence-strength constraints. Therefore, a high CUSUM score alone is not sufficient for final demotion. It must be supported by sufficient observations and confirmation evidence. This design allows the framework to benefit from temporal accumulation while maintaining safety against false positives.

\subsection{Bayesian Conservative Confirmation Gate}
\label{subsec:bayesian_conservative_gate}

The Bayesian conservative confirmation gate combines probabilistic trust
estimation with safety-oriented decision rules to prevent premature trust
enforcement. The gate requires sufficient accumulated evidence before a
vehicle is classified as malicious.

The conservative confirmation variable $G_i(t)$ represents additional safety
validation before final trust enforcement. It verifies whether sufficient
observation history and decision consistency exist for a potential BAN
action.

Let $h_i(t)\in\{0,1\}$ denote the handover consistency indicator, where
$h_i(t)=1$ indicates that the transferred trust state satisfies the required
continuity validation conditions.

\begin{equation}
G_i(t)=
\mathbb{I}\left[
n_i(t)\geq n_{\min}
\land w_i(t)\geq w_{\min}
\land h_i(t)=1
\right]
\label{eq:conservative_confirmation}
\end{equation}

where $w_i(t)$ represents accumulated warning history, $w_{\min}$ is the
minimum warning-history threshold, and $h_i(t)$ represents the handover
consistency indicator.

A vehicle is considered eligible for BAN only when multiple confirmation
conditions are satisfied. Let \(\Gamma_i(t)\) denote the confirmation
function:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Gamma_i(t)=\mathbb{I}\bigl[{}
&P_i^{m}(t)\geq \theta_B \land C_i(t)\geq H\\
&\land\ G_i(t)=1
\bigr],
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:confirmation_gate}
\end{equation}

where \(\theta_B\) is the Bayesian BAN threshold and \(G_i(t)\) denotes the
Bayesian conservative confirmation outcome incorporating observation-count,
warning-history, and handover-consistency validation. The indicator function
\(\mathbb{I}[\cdot]\) returns 1 only when all required conditions are
satisfied.

The role of \(G_i(t)\) is to represent conservative confirmation before final punishment. In practice, this means that a BAN decision must be supported not only by local suspicion but also by stronger decision consistency. If the evidence is suspicious but not sufficiently confirmed, the vehicle is assigned WARN rather than BAN.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:cusum_confirmation} summarizes guarded temporal accumulation and conservative confirmation.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Guarded CUSUM Accumulation and Conservative Confirmation}
\label{alg:cusum_confirmation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE $q_i(t)$, $P_i^{m}(t)$, $C_i(t-1)$, observation count $n_i(t)$, warning history $w_i(t)$, handover indicator $h_i(t)$
\STATE Update $C_i(t) \leftarrow \max(0,\, C_i(t-1)+q_i(t)-\nu)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:cusum_update}}
\IF{$C_i(t) \geq H$}
    \STATE Mark CUSUM alert active
\ENDIF
\STATE Compute $G_i(t) \leftarrow \mathbb{I}[n_i(t)\geq n_{\min} \land w_i(t)\geq w_{\min} \land h_i(t)=1]$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:conservative_confirmation}}
\STATE Compute $\Gamma_i(t) \leftarrow \mathbb{I}[P_i^{m}(t)\geq\theta_B \land C_i(t)\geq H \land G_i(t)=1]$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:confirmation_gate}}
\RETURN $C_i(t)$, $\Gamma_i(t)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Cross-UAV Trust Handover}
\label{subsec:trust_handover}

When a vehicle moves from one UAV zone to another, the trust evidence accumulated by the source UAV may become unavailable to the target UAV unless explicitly transferred. To prevent this loss of evidence, the proposed framework performs cross-UAV trust handover.

Let \(T_i^{u_a}(t^-)\) be the trust state of vehicle \(v_i\) at the source UAV \(u_a\) immediately before handover, and let \(T_i^{u_b}(t^+)\) be the initialized trust state at the target UAV \(u_b\) immediately after handover. The trust handover operation is defined as

\begin{equation}
T_i^{u_b}(t^+)=\mathcal{H}\left(T_i^{u_a}(t^-),\delta\right),
\label{eq:trust_handover}
\end{equation}

where \(\mathcal{H}(\cdot)\) is the handover function and \(\delta\) is the handover trust-discount parameter.

This mechanism allows the target UAV to continue trust evaluation with awareness of prior suspicious behavior rather than treating post-transition observations as disconnected evidence. This continuity provides substantial benefits when malicious evidence is distributed across UAV zones.

\subsection{Handover Trust Discounting}
\label{subsec:handover_discounting}

Directly transferring the full trust state may be unsafe because evidence collected in one zone may become stale or context-dependent after handover. Therefore, the proposed framework uses handover trust discounting. The discounted Bayesian evidence is computed as

\begin{equation}
\alpha_i^{u_b}(t^+) = 1 + \delta\left(\alpha_i^{u_a}(t^-)-1\right),
\label{eq:discount_alpha}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\beta_i^{u_b}(t^+) = 1 + \delta\left(\beta_i^{u_a}(t^-)-1\right),
\label{eq:discount_beta}
\end{equation}

where \(\delta\in[0,1]\). A smaller \(\delta\) reduces the influence of previous evidence, while a larger \(\delta\) transfers more history. This discounting prevents stale trust state from dominating future decisions while still preserving useful evidence across UAV boundaries.

The same principle is applied to warning and temporal suspicion history. Therefore, after handover, the target UAV starts with a cautious but informed trust state.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:handover_transfer} summarizes cross-UAV trust handover with discounting.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Cross-UAV Trust Handover with Discounting}
\label{alg:handover_transfer}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Source trust state $T_i^{u_a}(t^-)=(\alpha_i^{u_a},\beta_i^{u_a},w_i,C_i)$, discount factor $\delta$
\STATE Detect zone transition $u_a \rightarrow u_b$ for vehicle $v_i$
\STATE $\alpha_i^{u_b}(t^+) \leftarrow 1+\delta(\alpha_i^{u_a}(t^-)-1)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:discount_alpha}}
\STATE $\beta_i^{u_b}(t^+) \leftarrow 1+\delta(\beta_i^{u_a}(t^-)-1)$ \COMMENT{Eq.~\eqref{eq:discount_beta}}
\STATE Apply same discount $\delta$ to warning history $w_i$ and CUSUM state $C_i$
\STATE Set handover consistency indicator $h_i(t) \leftarrow 1$
\RETURN $T_i^{u_b}(t^+)=(\alpha_i^{u_b},\beta_i^{u_b},w_i^{u_b},C_i^{u_b})$, $h_i(t)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Impact of Handover-Aware Trust-State Continuity}

In highly dynamic UAV-assisted vehicular networks, a vehicle may transition between multiple UAV coverage regions during its communication lifetime. A conventional handover mechanism that initializes trust estimation independently at the newly associated UAV can result in loss of accumulated behavioral evidence and delayed suspicion confirmation. To address this limitation, the proposed framework performs handover-aware trust-state transfer, where decision-relevant trust variables, including Bayesian trust parameters $(\alpha,\beta)$, sequential suspicion statistics $(g^{+},g^{-})$, warning history, and confirmation status, are transferred through the cross-UAV trust-state manager.

Unlike a simple vehicle re-association mechanism, the proposed approach maintains the temporal continuity of trust evaluation while applying a controlled discount factor $\delta$ to accommodate possible changes in observation conditions. This allows the receiving UAV edge node to inherit previously accumulated evidence while preventing excessive confidence propagation from outdated observations.

The effectiveness of trust-state continuity was evaluated using a controlled handover-sensitive ablation experiment. The comparison was performed between the handover-enabled configuration and a configuration where trust-state continuity mechanisms were disabled. The results demonstrate that disabling trust continuity significantly reduces the ability of the system to maintain consistent detection decisions after UAV transitions, whereas the proposed cross-UAV trust-state continuity mechanism preserves detection capability without introducing additional false positives.

To further verify that the handover control mechanism remains operational
after the final implementation updates, an additional five-seed diagnostic
smoke test was conducted under an ablation-sensitive configuration. In this
diagnostic setting, the handover-enabled configuration achieved a detection
rate of $37.14\%$, whereas disabling the handover mechanism resulted in $0\%$
detection, with both configurations maintaining a false-positive rate of
$0\%$.

This diagnostic experiment is not used as the primary quantitative evaluation
of the handover contribution. Instead, its purpose is to confirm that the
handover control path remains active and that the absence of detection
differences observed in the final hard-evidence configuration is caused by
detector saturation rather than an inactive ablation mechanism. The primary
handover contribution is evaluated using the dedicated 15-seed handover
ablation experiment reported in Section~VI-B, where cross-UAV trust-state
continuity improves detection performance from $41.90\%$ to $96.19\%$ without
increasing false positives.

Furthermore, in the complete robustness evaluation using the final patched configuration, all experiments maintained zero fallback events and zero observed false positives, demonstrating that trust-state transfer operates conservatively and does not compromise safety during mobility-induced UAV transitions.

\subsection{Overlap BAN Guard}
\label{subsec:overlap_ban_guard}

During UAV overlap or handover regions, a vehicle may be observed under partial or ambiguous context. Weak BAN decisions in such regions can create false demotions, especially when evidence is inherited from another UAV or when packet loss reduces observation continuity. To address this issue, the proposed framework introduces an overlap BAN guard.

Let \(O_i(t)\in\{0,1\}\) indicate whether vehicle \(v_i\) is in an overlap or handover-sensitive region. Let \(P_i^{E}(t)\) denote the edge confidence score and \(P_i^{V}(t)\) denote the hard physical-evidence score. A weak overlap BAN is guarded when

\begin{equation}
O_i(t)=1
\land
P_i^{V}(t)<\theta_V
\land
P_i^{E}(t)<\theta_E,
\label{eq:overlap_guard_condition}
\end{equation}

where \(\theta_V\) and \(\theta_E\) are the hard-evidence and edge-confidence thresholds, respectively.

When this condition is satisfied, the decision is downgraded from BAN to WARN:

\begin{equation}
a_i(t)=\text{WARN} \quad \text{instead of} \quad \text{BAN}.
\label{eq:overlap_guard_action}
\end{equation}

This mechanism preserves safety by preventing weak overlap-region evidence from causing final demotion. It does not suppress strongly supported BAN decisions, because BAN remains possible when hard physical evidence or sufficient edge confidence is available.

\subsection{Decision Logic}
\label{subsec:decision_logic}

The final decision combines Bayesian suspicion, guarded CUSUM, confirmation, handover state, and overlap safety. The decision rule is

\begin{equation}
a_i(t)=
\begin{cases}
\text{BAN}, &
\begin{gathered}
\text{if } \Gamma_i(t)=1 \text{ and the}\\
\text{overlap guard is not triggered},
\end{gathered}\\
\text{WARN}, &
\begin{gathered}
\text{if } P_i^{m}(t)\geq \theta_W\\
\text{or } C_i(t)>0,
\end{gathered}\\
\text{SAFE}, & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\label{eq:decision_rule}
\end{equation}

where \(\theta_W\) is the warning threshold. This rule ensures that suspicious vehicles are monitored early through WARN decisions, but final demotion requires stronger confirmation.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:trust_decision} summarizes the final trust-decision logic.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Adaptive Trust Enforcement Procedure}
\label{alg:trust_decision}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Beacon evidence $\mathcal{B}_i(t)$
\FOR{each vehicle $i$}
    \STATE Evaluate confirmation gate $\Gamma_i(t)$
    \IF{$\Gamma_i(t) = 1$}
        \IF{$O_i(t)=1 \land P^V_i(t)<\theta_V \land P^E_i(t)<\theta_E$}
            \STATE $a_i(t) \leftarrow \text{\text{WARN}}$ \COMMENT{overlap BAN guard}
        \ELSE
            \STATE $a_i(t) \leftarrow \text{\text{BAN}}$
        \ENDIF
    \ELSIF{$P^m_i(t) \ge \theta_W \lor C_i(t) > 0$}
        \STATE $a_i(t) \leftarrow \text{\text{WARN}}$
    \ELSE
        \STATE $a_i(t) \leftarrow \text{\text{SAFE}}$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Design Rationale}
\label{subsec:design_rationale}

The proposed framework is designed around two constraints: detection effectiveness and safety preservation. Physical-consistency evidence provides immediate indicators of falsified mobility information, but it can be fragmented by handover and packet loss. Bayesian accumulation and guarded CUSUM address this limitation by integrating evidence over time. Cross-UAV trust handover prevents malicious vehicles from escaping suspicion by moving between UAV zones. Handover trust discounting prevents transferred evidence from becoming overly dominant. Finally, the overlap BAN guard protects honest vehicles from weak handover-region decisions.

Together, these mechanisms form a conservative but effective trust-management pipeline. The framework intentionally separates suspicion from punishment: suspicious evidence first produces WARN, while final BAN requires stronger confirmation. This design is particularly important for safety-critical vehicular systems, where false demotion of honest vehicles can reduce system reliability and trustworthiness.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experimental_methodology}

\subsection{Experimental Objectives}
\label{subsec:experimental_objectives}

The experimental evaluation was designed to assess whether the proposed multi-UAV edge--cloud trust framework can detect falsified vehicular messages while maintaining a low false-demotion rate for honest vehicles. The evaluation focuses on four questions. First, it examines whether preserving cross-UAV trust-state continuity improves detection when vehicles move across UAV coverage regions. Second, it evaluates whether guarded temporal evidence accumulation and Bayesian conservative confirmation improve malicious-vehicle demotion without increasing observed false-positive decisions. Third, it tests the robustness of the framework under packet loss, different beacon falsification probabilities, heterogeneous attack types, and increasing vehicle density. Finally, it compares the proposed framework against VeReMi-style physical-consistency baselines to determine whether mobility-aware trust continuity provides benefits when evidence is fragmented by handover and communication loss.

Unlike a standalone physical anomaly detector, the proposed framework addresses the broader trust-management problem in a mobile edge environment. Therefore, the evaluation reports not only detection rate, but also false-positive rate, clean-safety behavior, robustness, scalability, runtime overhead, and component-level ablation behavior.

\subsection{Simulation Environment}
\label{subsec:simulation_environment}

The experiments were conducted using an ns-3.41-based vehicular network simulation environment. The simulated scenario consists of connected vehicles moving through a road segment monitored by UAV-assisted edge nodes. Each vehicle periodically broadcasts mobility and status beacons containing timestamp, position, speed, heading, lane, and braking information. UAV edge nodes receive these beacons and evaluate their consistency using local evidence, trust history, and cloud/coordinator-assisted state transfer.

The system consists of four functional layers: a vehicle layer containing honest and malicious vehicles, a UAV edge layer that performs local trust evaluation, an edge--cloud bridge/coordinator layer that supports inter-zone state exchange, and a cloud layer that assists higher-level trust coordination. The UAV edge nodes generate trust decisions such as \textit{safe}, \textit{warn}, and \textit{ban}.

\subsection{Mobility, Handover, and Communication Model}
\label{subsec:mobility_handover_model}

The default scenario uses two UAV/edge zones covering adjacent road regions. As vehicles move along the road, they may transition from one UAV coverage zone to another. This transition creates a handover condition in which evidence collected by the previous UAV may be lost if trust state is not transferred. This property is central to the evaluation because a malicious vehicle can exploit zone transitions by spreading suspicious observations across multiple UAV segments.

The proposed framework addresses this issue using cross-UAV trust-state transfer. When a vehicle approaches or enters a new UAV zone, its trust evidence is transferred to the next edge node with handover trust discounting. The discounting mechanism avoids blindly carrying over stale evidence while still preserving useful suspicion history. Packet loss is emulated by dropping a controlled percentage of received telemetry packets. Communication reliability degradation is a critical factor in vehicular networks because packet loss can affect cooperative awareness and safety decisions~\cite{karagiannis2011vehicular}. This allows the evaluation to study the combined effect of handover and incomplete observations.

