\subsection{Baseline and Fair Comparison Protocol}
\label{subsec:baseline_protocol}

The proposed framework is compared with VeReMi-style
physical-consistency baselines because the objective of this work
is mobility-aware trust continuity rather than standalone message
classification. Learning-based misbehavior detectors provide
complementary classification capability but do not directly address
trust-state preservation during UAV handover. These baselines evaluate
whether received beacons violate mobility-consistency constraints, such as
timestamp continuity, position feasibility, speed consistency, and heading
behavior. The baseline is not treated as a complete trust-management
architecture. Rather, it represents the physical-evidence component commonly
used for falsified-message detection.

Two baseline forms are considered. The first is a centralized physical-consistency detector that has access to global vehicle history. This represents an upper-bound physical detector because it does not suffer from local UAV memory fragmentation. The second is a local segment-reset detector, where evidence is reset across contiguous UAV reception segments. This baseline is more relevant to the proposed problem because it reflects a practical UAV-local detector whose evidence can be fragmented by handover.

To ensure fairness, baseline thresholds are selected using clean-safety testing. An aggressive low threshold that produces false positives under clean honest conditions is excluded. The final comparison therefore uses the best clean-safe local segment-reset baseline. This prevents an unfair comparison in which a baseline achieves high detection only by falsely demoting honest vehicles.

