\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{subsec:evaluation_metrics}

The primary detection metric is detection rate (DR), defined as

\begin{equation}
DR = \frac{TP}{TP+FN} \times 100,
\label{eq:dr}
\end{equation}

where $TP$ is the number of malicious vehicles correctly demoted and $FN$ is the number of malicious vehicles not demoted.

The primary safety metric is false-positive rate (FPR), defined as

\begin{equation}
FPR = \frac{FP}{FP+TN} \times 100,
\label{eq:fpr}
\end{equation}

where $FP$ is the number of honest vehicles falsely demoted and $TN$ is the number of honest vehicles correctly preserved.

A malicious vehicle is counted as detected when its final evaluation log
reports \texttt{demoted=YES}. An honest vehicle with \texttt{demoted=YES} is
counted as a false positive. In addition to DR and FPR, the evaluation reports
pooled counts, per-seed mean and standard deviation, confidence intervals where
applicable, fallback count, and runtime overhead. False-positive behavior is
treated as a first-class metric because false demotion of honest vehicles can
degrade the safety and reliability of cooperative vehicular systems.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis and Reproducibility}
\label{subsec:statistical_analysis}

For primary experiments, results are aggregated over 15 independent random seeds. Both pooled statistics and per-seed statistics are reported. Pooled statistics reflect the total number of detected malicious vehicles and falsely demoted honest vehicles across all seeds. Per-seed statistics capture variability across randomized simulation runs.

For baseline comparison, paired statistical tests and confidence intervals are used where appropriate. In particular, paired detection differences are analyzed using seed-level comparisons, and detection disagreements between the proposed framework and the local segment-reset baseline are assessed using exact paired tests. This allows the comparison to distinguish between numerical improvement and statistically supported improvement.

All final experiment outputs reported in this paper are generated from locked structured CSV files and are associated with raw logs and checksum records. Each reported table is traceable to a locked result file. This locking procedure reduces ambiguity between exploratory runs, diagnostic runs, and final reported results.

\subsection{Treatment of Ablations and Final-Code Consistency}
\label{subsec:ablation_treatment}

The evaluation distinguishes between dedicated component ablations and final-code consistency checks. Dedicated handover and CUSUM ablations are used to isolate the contribution of each mechanism. These ablations are performed under configurations that expose the effect of removing the component under study.

To verify that the ablation isolates trust-state continuity rather than
vehicle mobility, simulation traces were inspected after execution. The
handover-disabled configuration produced explicit Z1-to-Z2 cold-start
events while preserving vehicle transitions. Across the evaluated runs,
145 cold-start transition events were recorded, confirming that the
ablation removes inherited trust information rather than suppressing UAV
association changes.

The final patched implementation introduces stronger hard-evidence support and the overlap BAN guard. Under this final hard-evidence configuration, the base adaptive scenario can exhibit a ceiling effect: physical evidence alone may be sufficient to detect nearly all malicious vehicles, so disabling handover or CUSUM may not reduce detection. Therefore, the final-code consistency rerun is not used as the main handover or CUSUM ablation. Instead, it is used to verify implementation stability, clean safety, and absence of fallback behavior.

To confirm that the ON/OFF controls remain active in the patched code, an additional five-seed diagnostic smoke test is conducted under an ablation-sensitive configuration. This smoke test restores the expected ON/OFF separation and confirms that the no-difference behavior in the final hard-evidence rerun is due to detector saturation rather than inactive ablation controls.

\subsection{Evaluation Logic}
\label{subsec:evaluation_logic}

The evaluation follows a layered methodology. First, clean-safety experiments verify that the system does not demote honest vehicles in benign conditions. Second, dedicated ablation experiments isolate the effects of handover and guarded CUSUM. Third, packet-loss, beacon-falsification-probability, attack-variant, and scalability experiments evaluate robustness under increasingly challenging conditions. Fourth, the proposed framework is compared against a clean-safe local segment-reset VeReMi-style baseline to determine the value of mobility-aware trust continuity. Finally, overlap-guard and final-code smoke tests verify that the final patched implementation improves safety and remains operationally stable.

This methodology supports the central claim that the proposed framework is not merely a physical anomaly detector. It is a mobility-aware multi-UAV trust-management system that accumulates, transfers, confirms, and enforces trust decisions under fragmented and lossy observations.

