\subsection{Overall Discussion}
\label{subsec:overall_discussion}

The experimental results collectively demonstrate that reliable falsified
vehicle-beacon detection in multi-UAV environments requires more than
instantaneous anomaly identification. The proposed framework combines
physical-consistency evidence, Bayesian trust evolution, temporal suspicion
accumulation, conservative confirmation, and cross-UAV trust-state continuity
to address the challenges introduced by vehicle mobility and fragmented
observations.

The ablation studies provide direct evidence regarding the contribution of
individual design components. The handover evaluation shows that preserving
trust-state continuity across UAV boundaries substantially improves detection
capability by allowing the receiving UAV to exploit previously accumulated
behavioral evidence. Without trust-state transfer, detection performance
degrades significantly because fragmented observations are evaluated
independently.

Similarly, the guarded CUSUM analysis demonstrates that temporal evidence
accumulation improves decision reliability compared with relying only on
instantaneous trust estimation. By requiring persistent suspicious behavior
before enforcement, the framework improves detection sensitivity while
maintaining zero observed false positives.

The robustness experiments further indicate that the proposed framework
remains effective under simulated communication degradation and adversarial conditions.
The framework maintains high detection performance under packet-loss
conditions up to 20\% and preserves zero observed false positives across the evaluated
beacon falsification probabilities. This behavior is attributed to the complementary
interaction between Bayesian trust updating and sequential suspicion
tracking, which reduces the dependence on any single observation.

Comparison with the local segment-reset VeReMi-style baseline highlights the
importance of maintaining historical trust information in mobile UAV
environments. While both approaches perform similarly under ideal
communication conditions, the proposed framework provides improved robustness
under degraded communication conditions because historical trust evidence
remains available across UAV transitions.

Finally, scalability evaluation reveals the inherent trade-off between
network size and detection performance. Increasing vehicle density introduces
more concurrent evidence streams and reduces individual observation
opportunities, resulting in reduced detection rate and increased runtime.
Nevertheless, the framework maintains conservative safety behavior with zero
observed false positives across the evaluated network sizes.

Overall, the results support the hypothesis that mobility-aware trust
continuity combined with temporal evidence verification provides a practical
approach for improving dependability of distributed UAV-assisted
vehicular networks.

Considering all experiment categories, vehicle-density settings,
packet-loss configurations, attack variants, and ablation studies,
the evaluation represents a systematic multi-scenario simulation campaign rather
than isolated executions. Each configuration was independently
executed across 15 seeds, producing reproducible performance
statistics from multiple long-duration ns-3 simulation runs.

