\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{sec:limitations_future_work}

\textit{Limitations:}
Although the proposed framework demonstrates strong performance in the
evaluated scenarios, the current study relies on simulation-based validation.
The impact of environmental uncertainty, UAV positioning errors, hardware
limitations, and large-scale communication interference requires further
investigation through field deployment studies.

Although the proposed framework includes a cloud-based intelligence layer for
global aggregation and policy dissemination, the current experimental
evaluation focuses on edge-level UAV computations. The active role of the
cloud in real-time decision-making is left for future exploration.

First, detection performance decreases as vehicle density increases. In the scalability experiment, the framework preserves zero observed false positives across all tested densities, but the detection rate decreases from 96.19\% at 10 vehicles to 52.29\% at 50 vehicles. This indicates that the current implementation is conservative under dense traffic and that each vehicle receives fewer effective observations as the monitoring load increases. Future work should investigate density-aware UAV watcher allocation, adaptive evidence scheduling, and dynamic load balancing across UAV edge nodes.

Second, runtime increases super-linearly with vehicle count. The mean runtime increases from 86.73 s at 10 vehicles to 1189.40 s at 50 vehicles. Although the framework remains safety-preserving, this runtime behavior limits scalability for larger deployments. Future implementations should optimize trust-state storage, reduce redundant edge--cloud communication, and explore parallelized or event-triggered evidence processing.

Third, timestamp-only falsification remains more difficult under packet loss than position- or speed-dominant falsification. In the attack-variant evaluation, timestamp-only detection decreases from 92.38\% under no packet loss to 49.52\% under 20\% packet loss, while the framework still preserves zero observed false positives. This suggests that temporal-only attacks require stronger timing-specific evidence models, such as clock-drift profiling, inter-arrival-time consistency, or multi-observer temporal correlation.

Furthermore, highly intermittent attackers that strategically alternate
between benign and malicious transmissions may reduce the effectiveness of
sequential evidence accumulation by preventing sustained suspicion growth.
Reputation-then-defect behaviors and adaptive pacing strategies represent
important adversarial cases that will be investigated in future work through
adaptive attack modeling and learning-assisted defense mechanisms.

Fourth, the primary evaluation uses a two-UAV handover scenario to isolate the effect of trust-state transfer. This design is suitable for controlled analysis, but larger deployments may involve multiple UAVs, non-uniform coverage, overlapping handover regions, UAV mobility constraints, and dynamic UAV repositioning. Future work should extend the framework to multi-hop UAV handover, heterogeneous UAV coverage, and cooperative UAV placement.

Fifth, the attacker model focuses on falsified vehicular beacon content and assumes that UAV edge nodes, the edge--cloud coordinator, and the trust-management software are not compromised. This is appropriate for studying message-level falsification attacks, but future work should consider stronger adversaries, including colluding vehicles, compromised edge nodes, Sybil attacks, coordinated replay, and adaptive attackers that learn the trust thresholds over time.

Finally, the current framework is validated using ns-3-based simulation. Although simulation enables controlled ablation, repeatability, and stress testing, field trials or hardware-in-the-loop validation would provide stronger evidence for deployment readiness. Future studies should therefore evaluate the framework with real V2X traces, UAV-assisted testbeds, and mixed simulation--emulation environments.

Although learning-based misbehavior detectors have demonstrated
high classification accuracy, they primarily optimize message-level
classification performance. The objective of this work is different:
maintaining trust-state continuity across UAV handover under
fragmented observations. Therefore, direct comparison with ML
classifiers is outside the scope of this study. Future work will
investigate hybrid trust-learning approaches that combine temporal
trust evolution with data-driven classifiers.

These limitations do not weaken the main contribution of this work. Rather, they identify the next research steps required to move from a controlled mobility-aware trust-management framework toward a scalable and deployment-ready UAV-assisted vehicular security system.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper presented a mobility-aware multi-UAV edge--cloud trust-management
framework for improving the dependability of vehicular communication under
falsified-beacon attacks. Unlike conventional detection approaches that
operate on isolated observations, the proposed framework integrates
multi-source evidence fusion, Bayesian trust evolution, guarded temporal
suspicion tracking, conservative trust confirmation, and cross-UAV
trust-state continuity to maintain consistent security decisions during
vehicle mobility.

The experimental evaluation demonstrated that the proposed
framework achieves a detection rate of 96.19\% with zero observed
false positives across the evaluated ns-3 simulation scenarios.
The ablation analysis confirmed the contribution of individual design
components. Preserving cross-UAV trust-state continuity improved detection
capability from 41.90\% to 96.19\%, demonstrating the importance of retaining
decision-relevant trust evidence when vehicles transition between UAV
coverage regions. Similarly, integrating guarded CUSUM-based temporal
verification increased detection capability from 71.43\% to 96.19\% while
maintaining zero observed false positives.

The robustness evaluation further showed that the proposed framework remains
effective under realistic communication degradation. Under 20\% packet loss,
the framework maintained 91.43\% detection performance with zero observed
false positives. Compared with a clean-safe local segment-reset VeReMi-style
baseline, the proposed approach provided improved resilience under degraded
communication conditions, achieving a 12.38 percentage-point detection
advantage at 20\% packet loss by preserving historical trust information
across UAV transitions.

The scalability evaluation revealed the expected trade-off between network
density and detection performance. While increasing the number of vehicles
from 10 to 50 introduced higher computational overhead and reduced detection
capability due to increased evidence contention, the framework maintained
zero observed false positives throughout the evaluated scenarios. This
highlights the importance of balancing detection sensitivity, computational
cost, and conservative trust enforcement in large-scale UAV-assisted
vehicular networks.

Despite these advantages, the current evaluation is limited to simulation
experiments with predefined mobility and attack models. Future work will
focus on real-world UAV--vehicle deployment validation, adaptive trust
parameter optimization, larger-scale heterogeneous UAV networks, and
learning-assisted mechanisms for improving scalability while preserving
interpretability and safety-preserving behavior.

Overall, the results demonstrate that mobility-aware trust-state continuity
combined with temporal evidence verification provides a promising approach
for enhancing the dependability of distributed UAV-assisted vehicular
networks against falsified information attacks.





